<!-- TEMPLATE: every exercise below is a placeholder. No questions have been
     chosen yet, and the page headings are provisional.
     Workshop 7 covers Week 8 videos 4-6 (polar form and de Moivre's theorem,
     exponential form, logarithms of complex numbers) and Week 9 videos 1-3
     (nth roots, multiple-angle formulae, hyperbolic functions). Week 9
     videos 4-5 (powers of sine and cosine, sine and cosine of a complex
     number) come after this workshop.
     Notation follows the lecture slides: j, z = x + jy, the polar form
     r(cos(theta) + j sin(theta)), the exponential form r e^{j theta},
     modulus |z|, argument arg z with principal value in (-pi, pi], radians
     throughout, ln z = ln|z| + j(arg z + 2n pi), "nth roots", cosh, sinh,
     tanh and Osborn's rule. Numbers are written 2 + 3j; j goes first only
     before a letter or a surd.
     The slides call e^{j pi} + 1 = 0 "Euler's formula" and the formula sheet
     calls e^{j theta} = cos(theta) + j sin(theta) "Euler's relations", so
     say "exponential form" and write the relation out.
     Plan: about 30 minutes of core work, counting discussion parts double,
     plus an optional challenge. Each page heading names the highest Bloom
     level that page reaches; the warm-up stays at Remember and Understand.
     Settled so far (16 Nov 2026): Exercise 1 mixes points read from an
     Argand diagram with numbers given as a + bj; Exercise 5 is the cube
     roots of 8j; Exercise 6 has no Osborn's rule part; the challenge is
     about mains electricity.
     There is no slot for multiple-angle formulae (Week 9 video 2). They
     could wait for Workshop 8 alongside powers of sine and cosine (Week 9
     video 4). -->
<pretext xml:lang="en-US" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <xi:include href="docinfo.ptx"/>
  <article xml:id="worksheet07wks">
    <title>Engineering Maths 1</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>University of Bristol — EMAT10100</p>
      <p>Use the printer icon for a copy with working space. Give exact answers unless stated otherwise.</p>
    </introduction>
    <worksheet>
      <title>Workshop 7: Polar form, roots and hyperbolic functions</title>
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Warm-up</title>
          <p>Please work in collaboration with your classmates to complete the following exercises. This means sharing ideas and asking each other questions.</p>
        </assemblage>
        <!-- Understand, then Apply (Week 8 videos 4-5). Four numbers to
             write in exponential form, one per task: two are points on an
             Argand diagram, which is the data here and still to be drawn,
             and two are given as a + bj. The numbers return in Exercises
             3, 4 and 5. The "-twocol" id sets the tasks two to a row
             (assets/custom.css), so each number has its own space. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w7-forms-twocol">
          <introduction>
            <p>[Placeholder: an Argand diagram with two labelled points, and one instruction to write four numbers in exponential form.]</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="1.0in">
            <statement><p>[Placeholder: point A.]</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.0in">
            <statement><p>[Placeholder: point B.]</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.0in">
            <statement><p>[Placeholder: a + bj form.]</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.0in">
            <statement><p>[Placeholder: a + bj form.]</p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <!-- Apply. One short question reviewing Week 8 videos 1-3 (complex
             roots of a real quadratic), with the roots written in
             exponential form. They return in Exercise 5(c) and in the
             challenge. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w7-review" workspace="1.4in">
          <statement>
            <p>[Placeholder: one short review question from last week, reused later on the sheet.]</p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
      </page>
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Analyse: Products, Quotients and Powers</title>
        </assemblage>
        <!-- Apply, then Analyse (Week 8 video 4). Multiply and divide in
             polar form, take a power with de Moivre's theorem, then say
             what multiplying by a complex number does on the Argand
             diagram. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w7-polar">
          <introduction>
            <p>[Placeholder: set-up that reuses the numbers from Exercise 1.]</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="1.6in">
            <statement><p>[Placeholder: a product or a quotient in polar form.]</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.6in">
            <statement><p>[Placeholder: a power, by de Moivre's theorem.]</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.4in">
            <statement><p>[Placeholder: explain what multiplying does on the Argand diagram.]</p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Evaluate: Arguments and Logarithms</title>
        </assemblage>
        <!-- Apply, then Evaluate (Week 8 videos 4 and 6). A logarithm of a
             complex number with all of its values, then two students'
             claims built on a real misconception about the principal
             value. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w7-logs">
          <introduction>
            <p>[Placeholder: set-up for the logarithm of a complex number.]</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="2.0in">
            <statement><p>[Placeholder: find all the values of a logarithm and mark the principal value.]</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="2.2in">
            <statement><p>[Placeholder: two students' claims about arguments or logarithms. Explain in your own words what each student has got wrong.]</p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Analyse: Roots of Complex Numbers</title>
        </assemblage>
        <!-- Apply, then Analyse (Week 9 video 1). The three cube roots of
             8j, one of which Exercise 3 has already found. Plot them on the
             blank Argand diagram, then use the pattern: what takes each
             root to the next, and what the three add up to (used again in
             the challenge). The diagram is blank axes for the students' own
             plot, not a figure of the answer. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w7-roots">
          <introduction>
            <p>[Placeholder: set-up for the cube roots of 8j.]</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="2.0in">
            <statement><p>[Placeholder: find all three cube roots.]</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="0in">
            <statement>
              <p>[Placeholder: plot the roots on this Argand diagram.]</p>
              <image source="blank-argand.svg" width="42%">
                <description>Blank Argand diagram: a horizontal real axis labelled Re and a vertical imaginary axis labelled Im crossing at the centre, with no scale marked.</description>
              </image>
            </statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.2in">
            <statement><p>[Placeholder: a question about the pattern that the roots make.]</p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Create: Hyperbolic Functions</title>
        </assemblage>
        <!-- Apply, then Create (Week 9 video 3). Work from the definitions
             of cosh and sinh, then write an example for a neighbour to
             check. Osborn's rule was dropped from this sheet to save
             time. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w7-hyperbolic">
          <introduction>
            <p>[Placeholder: set-up for the hyperbolic functions.]</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="2.0in">
            <statement><p>[Placeholder: a calculation from the definitions of cosh and sinh.]</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="2.0in">
            <statement><p>[Placeholder: write down your own example, then swap with a neighbour and check each other's.]</p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Challenge: Mains Electricity</title>
        </assemblage>
        <!-- Deep. Britain's mains supply is three-phase at 50 Hz: three
             voltages a third of a cycle apart. With w = e^{2 pi j/3} from
             Exercise 2, 1 + w + w^2 = 0, so the three voltages add up to
             zero at every instant. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w7-challenge">
          <introduction>
            <p>[Placeholder: the three voltages of a three-phase mains supply.]</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="2.6in">
            <statement><p>[Placeholder: part (a).]</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.8in">
            <statement><p>[Placeholder: part (b).]</p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>
    </worksheet>
